f70a2e21ff266ba5b71495a23ae2.png)\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L8}$ and $\\textrm{L9}.$ Starting from $\\textrm{L8},$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{At-At_0}\\mathbf{x}(t_0),\n$$\n\nwe rewrite the exponent using the algebraic identity $At-At_0=A(t-t_0)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\textrm{L9}.$ Thus, $\\textrm{L9}$ follows from $\\textrm{L8}$ by the fact that $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt lightgray]{At-At_0=A(t-t_0)\\text{ for any }t\\text{ and }t_0}.$" 13194139535845 true] [17592186228869 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/assets/c9fe7104001d89b4266ab760530a1db5abee0cb9206a53d374827892db114296.png)\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/assets/bf7bc97a737f653b03369fa91a29f564703abf6172aba7ba6db62acae12c1af4.png) where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/assets/d63e1f20fcf33dd0ba20d1ba2be8ae008888e794c0054da8b8628f182c501068.png)*" 13194139535845 true] [17592186228869 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = 7y(t); y^{′}(t) = 7x(t)$,\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (7t) & \\sinh (7t) \\\\ \\sinh (7t) & \\cosh (7t)\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$*" 13194139535845 false] [17592186228869 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/assets/cdd98c904dce4f0beebc1333ce00f3f54551a93eddb5a8e52af6392946c2f910.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/assets/2dff913b6a0163c9dff6e28d81690472f043de69e4796dffb58b16b933165450.png) So, the matrix of the system is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/assets/b6e79ba4308d37fec08efa34e15e009e938fc6df4bceba677837897c21602397.png)\n\nGiven the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\mathbf{c} =e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t = t_0,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = e^{At_0} \\, \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nand since $e^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = e^{-At_0} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $\\mathbf{x}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7(t-1)) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535845 true] [17592186228869 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\mathbf{c} =e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nis the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t = t_0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = e^{At_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nand since $e^{At}$ is invertible for all $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = e^{-At_0} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $\\mathbf{x}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{At}e^{-At_{0}}x(t_{0}) \\\\\n&= e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(t_{0})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{A(t - 1)}x(1) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (7(t - 1)) & \\sinh (7(t - 1)) \\\\ \\sinh (7(t - 1)) & \\cosh (7(t - 1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sinh (7(t - 1)) \\\\ 3\\cosh (7(t - 1))\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535845 false] [17592186228870 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/assets/ddc88d8b24bc6bc4ea37107bc2419d3e3bd2607c1135531c01ce9ae85bc2b5c7.png)\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/assets/5ad7137465faba80d0da35cf68c609af07cd3241740a6f7e2163a6dd0653884b.png) where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071035/assets/fddbe93ca2141bf2590d292fb0d2b3ca9520bb33c531ee03d9431b92617da3e2.png)*" 13194139535845 true] [17592186228870 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = 3y(t); y^{′}(t) =-3x(t)$,\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) & \\sin (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t) & \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$*" 13194