$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ $\\text{by substituting }t={t}_{0}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Line $\\text{L5}$ gives\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c.\n$$\n\nSince $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={I}_{n}$, the right-hand side simplifies to $c$, so\n\n$$\nc=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nSimplify once, then apply $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$, to obtain\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L6}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can use the property \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7},$ we get line $\\text{L8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369813 13194139535845 true] [17592186369812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369814 13194139535845 true] [17592186369812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369815 13194139535845 true] [17592186369812 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535845 true] [17592186369812 1041 "field-1" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369812 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369813 13194139535845 true] [17592186369812 1110 #uuid "475fcb80-1424-5462-a360-cae20cbfd378" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369813 1034 "by substituting $t={t}_{0}$" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369813 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535845 true] [17592186369813 1111 #uuid "9756b0dd-50d5-50ec-800e-ae163a7c5e97" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369814 1034 "using the fact that ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$" 13194139535845 tr