$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by {{field-3}}." 13194139535845 true] [17592186369837 1113 #uuid "0d9118b1-b0ff-5db4-a05c-ec3ad938a6f1" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369837 1145 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Starting from $\\text{L4},$\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwe multiply both sides on the left by $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}$ to get\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L5}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides on the left by }({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L7}.$ Using line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nFrom line $\\text{L6}$, we know that $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}).$ Substituting this expression for $c$ into the formula for $x(t)$ gives\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can use the property \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7},$ we get line $\\text{L8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369839 13194139535845 true] [17592186369838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369840 13194139535845 true] [17592186369838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369841 13194139535845 true] [17592186369838 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535845 true] [17592186369838 1041 "field-1" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369838 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369841 13194139535845 true] [17592186369838 1110 #uuid "3eb5f316-9ea3-5d32-836d-27026f9f73b5" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369839 1034 "integrating $x(t)$" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369839 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535845 true] [17592186369839 1111 #uuid "4c24a775-a2ea-5869-989e-08449ae4d3ec" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369840 1034 "using the identity $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535845 true] [17592186369840 1111 #uuid "b866f10c-468e-5891-ab43-7f8357cc8b01" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369841 1034 "multiplying both sides on the left by $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}$" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369841 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535845 true] [17592186369841 1111 #uuid "e52d0878-989b-508d-a171-20e42a5f12ea" 13194139535845 true] [17592186369842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369843 13194139535845 true] [17592186